# Reading Input from a USB RFID Card Reader

In the realm of technology, the simplicity of a device can often mask its profound capabilities. Recently, a compelling discussion emerged on Hacker News regarding a low-cost RFID reader that operates seamlessly as a keyboard when plugged into a computer. This device has sparked the interest of tech enthusiasts eager to integrate RFID technology into their applications.

## Understanding the RFID Reader

The RFID reader in question is noteworthy for its ability to read RFID cards or tokens and directly input the values into any application, much like a traditional keyboard. However, as discussions unfolded, users sought clarity on the specific model used in the article, noting a potential confusion with barcode scanning technology. 

One user expressed interest in identifying an RFID reader that functions similarly, prompting valuable suggestions from the community. For instance, the **Chameleon Ultra V2.0**, an open-source project, was recommended. This versatile device can be configured to operate as a Human Interface Device (HID), allowing users to build their own or purchase pre-built options that meet their needs.

## Technical Implementation

The author of the original article, Kevwe Technology AB, opted for a more technical approach, choosing to implement the solution in Golang rather than utilizing a Python library. This preference highlights a growing trend among developers who seek robust performance and control over their software environments.

The key to capturing input from the RFID reader lies in the use of the `EVIOCGRAB` command, which allows exclusive access to the input device. The provided code snippet is a straightforward Golang program that continuously reads input from the RFID reader, effectively translating the scanned data into usable application input when an Enter key event is detected. 

